{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## DECISION-3B5F-C: prior art INVERTS part of B. Restoring same-pass resurrection may be optimising the wrong behaviour.\n\nChecked the re-home plan against how actively-maintained Jira integrations actually handle this. Three\nfindings, and the second one contradicts DECISION-3B5F-B's plan.\n\n### 1. Detection channel: the industry uses WEBHOOKS; polling \"often misses deletions\"\n\nMature integrations subscribe to Jira's `jira:issue_deleted` event rather than inferring deletion from\na polled working set. Atlassian's own webhook docs and integration guides make the point that polling\ncatches new and edited issues but deletions \"slip through the cracks\".\n\n**This does not indict rebar's approach**, and one caveat actively vindicates it: deleting a PROJECT\nfrom the UI does **not** fire issue-deleted webhooks, so even webhook-driven tools need a\nreconciliation backstop. rebar is a git-backed CLI with no webhook receiver, so a bounded-GET probe is\nthe correct shape for its architecture — it is the fallback the webhook tools also need, not a\nsubstitute they avoided.\n\n### 2. Re-creating a deleted item: the leading commercial syncer DELIBERATELY REFUSES TO\n\nUnito, on moved/deleted work items, states plainly that it **will not recreate deleted work items**,\n\"assuming that once a work item is deleted, you don't want it back\", and that this exists specifically\nto **prevent flows from creating copies of work items you want to stay deleted**.\n\nThat is a considered rejection of exactly what c244 does. Kubernetes-style reconcilers land in the same\nplace from a different direction: the idiomatic handling of a missing object is `IgnoreNotFound` —\n\"controllers shouldn't try to determine *why* a resource is missing … simply observe that it's missing\"\n— never resurrect it.\n\n**So DECISION-3B5F-B was about to restore, faster, a behaviour that the closest commercial analogue\ntreats as a defect to guard against.** \"The ADR promises same-pass re-create and we lost it\" is true,\nand the right response is not automatically to give it back.\n\n### 3. rebar's DISCRIMINATION is more conservative than Kubernetes — and correctly so\n\nK8s can treat \"absent from cache\" as gone because the informer cache is *intended* to be complete.\nrebar's working set is **deliberately incomplete**: ADR 0028 L17's split-JQL caps the Done window, so\nabsence is expected for live issues. That asymmetry is exactly why the four-way\n`observe_jira` (present / confirmed-404 / absent-in-window / transport-error) is justified where a K8s\ncontroller needs no such thing. This part of the design is *better* than a naive port of the reconciler\npattern, and nothing here argues against it.\n\n### The inversion, stated plainly\n\nrebar today **already resurrects** a hard-deleted issue — just slowly. Measured earlier: 3 consecutive\nconfirmed 404s retire the binding (`_retire` pops it from `bindings` and `reverse`), the local ticket\nbecomes unbound, and `outbound_differ.py:502-513` creates a fresh Jira issue. So the \"deficiency\" of\nc244 being dead is **not** that rebar fails to resurrect; it is that rebar resurrects on a 3-pass delay\ninstead of immediately. Both are the behaviour Unito exists to prevent.\n\nWhich makes the real question not \"same-pass or eventual?\" but **\"should a confirmed hard-delete\nresurrect at all?\"** That is a product decision about whose intent wins — the operator who deleted the\nJira issue, or the local store that still holds the ticket — and it is not answerable from the code.\n\n### Revised recommendation\n\n1. **Delete the dead legacy arm.** Uncontested by all three findings, and unchanged from A and B.\n2. **Do NOT restore same-pass resurrection.** Making an arguably-wrong behaviour faster is the worst of\n   the available moves.\n3. **Escalate the real question:** should a confirmed hard-delete resurrect? Prior art says no by\n   default. The likely right shape is an explicit policy — resurrect vs. tombstone-and-stop-syncing —\n   defaulting to NOT resurrecting, with the local ticket marked so the divergence is visible rather\n   than silently re-created. That is a larger design change than this ticket and wants its own ADR\n   amendment, because it also governs the EXISTING eventual-resurrection path, which no ticket has ever\n   examined.\n4. **Correct ADR 0028 either way.** Its Consequences assert c244 works; it does not. Leaving that\n   uncorrected is how the next reader re-derives this from scratch.\n\n### What I am NOT claiming\n\nI have not audited Exalate's or sync2jira's delete semantics at code level, and the searches did not\nsurface Exalate's specific behaviour — so \"the industry\" here rests on Unito's documented behaviour\nplus the Kubernetes reconciler convention, not on a survey. Treat item 2 as strong evidence, not\nsettled consensus.\n\nSources: Unito on moved/deleted work items (guide.unito.io/how-does-moving-work-items-work-with-unito);\nAtlassian webhooks (developer.atlassian.com/cloud/jira/platform/webhooks/,\ndeveloper.atlassian.com/server/jira/platform/webhooks/); kubebuilder finalizers\n(book.kubebuilder.io/reference/using-finalizers); controller-runtime reconcile\n(pkg.go.dev/sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime/pkg/reconcile)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785869035847098001,"uuid":"c09c56f4-8e3f-4bd3-b40c-c549de52086d"}